const ALL96 = `This is the Sagrada Familia in Spain. It is one of the most famous churches in the world. Look at the beautiful tall columns inside the church. They look like trees, don't they? The famous architect, Antoni Gaudi, used the shape of tress in the Sagrada Familia. That's how he brought the beauty of nature (A)[indoors / outdoors].\nJørn Utzon, the architect of the Sydney Opera House, took a shape from nature and added his imagination. Can you guess what inspired him? Many people think that it is the waves in the ocean or a sailing boat. But interestingly, the (B)[exhibition / inspiration] came from an orange. Look at the roof closely. Can you see the peels of an orange? When orange lights are shone on the building, you can see the peels more clearly.\nWhat about Korea? Have you ever been to Dongdaemun Design Plaza in Seoul? Many people think that the building looks like a giant spaceship. But the architect, Zaha Hadid, took the curved lines from nature so that city people could enjoy them. (C)[Because of / Without] its special design, it has become a popular tourist attraction in Seoul.`;
add(R, passage(P, ALL96, `위 글의 괄호 (A), (B), (C)에서 흐름상 알맞은 말로 바르게 연결된 것은?`), '1', ['(A) indoors – (B) inspiration – (C) Because of', '(A) indoors – (B) exhibition – (C) Because of', '(A) indoors – (B) inspiration – (C) Without', '(A) outdoors – (B) inspiration – (C) Because of', '(A) outdoors – (B) exhibition – (C) Without']);
add(R, passage(P, ALL96, `위 글의 내용과 일치하는 것은?`), '4', ['Antoni Gaudi made tall columns outside the church.', 'Jørn Utzon was inspired by the waves when he designed the Sydney Opera House.', 'The Sydney Opera House is one of the largest sailing boats in the world.', 'The architect, Zaha Hadid, wanted city people to enjoy the curved lines from nature.', 'Dongdaemun Design Plaza is only known to just a few tourists in Seoul.']);
const SAG98 = `Nature has inspired many architects around the world. ⓐ<u>This</u> is the Sagrada Familia in Spain. ⓑ<u>It</u> is one of the most famous churches in the world. Look at the beautiful tall columns inside ⓒ<u>the church</u>. They look like trees, don't they? The famous architect, Antoni Gaudi, used the shape of tress in ⓓ<u>the Sagrada Familia</u>. That's how he brought ⓔ<u>the beauty of nature</u> indoors.`;
add(R, passage(P, SAG98, `위 글의 밑줄 친 ⓐ~ⓔ 중, 가리키는 대상이 다른 하나는?`), '5', CIRCLE);
add(R, passage(P, SAG98, `위 글의 the Sagrada Familia에 대한 설명으로 옳지 않은 것은?`), '3', ['It was designed by Antoni Gaudi.', 'It is well known all around the world.', "You're not allowed to go inside the church.", 'Gaudi was inspired by nature when he designed it.', 'Gaudi took the shape of trees for the columns inside the church.']);
add(R, `다음 주어진 글에 이어질 순서로 알맞은 것은?\n\nHave you heard of the expression, "Art imitates nature?" Many artists get their ideas and inspirations from the world around them.\n\n(A) Can you imagine a building that looks like an egg? Such a building actually exists in London.\n(B) This is because the natural world is a beautiful place. The shapes in nature are very pleasing to the eye.\n(C) For example, look at the shape of an egg. Isn't it beautiful? It is round and delicate, yet strong enough to protect its contents.`, '4', ['(A)-(B)-(C)', '(A)-(C)-(B)', '(B)-(A)-(C)', '(B)-(C)-(A)', '(C)-(B)-(A)']);
const SYDDP101 = `In the first two examples, we can ⓐ________ see what inspired the architect. But in the next example from Australia, this is not so ⓑ________. Jørn Utzon, the architect of the Sydney Opera House, took a shape from nature and added his imagination. Can you guess what inspired him? Many people think that it is the waves in the ocean or a sailing boat. But interestingly, the inspiration came from an orange. Look at the roof ⓒ________. Can you see the peels of an orange? When orange lights are shone on the building, you can see the peels more ⓓ________.\nWhat about Korea? Have you ever been to Dongdaemun Design Plaza in Seoul? Many people think that the building looks like a giant spaceship. But the architect, Zaha Hadid, took the curved lines from nature so that city people could enjoy them. Thanks to its special design, it has become a popular tourist ⓔ________ in Seoul.`;
add(R, passage(P, SYDDP101, `위 글의 내용과 일치하는 것은? (정답 2개)`), '2, 4', ['Zaha Hadid는 Sydney Opera House의 건축가다.', 'Dongdaemun Design Plaza는 자연의 곡선을 본 따서 만들었다.', 'Jørn Utzon은 바다의 파도에서 영감을 얻어 건축물을 만들었다.', '사람들은 Sydney Opera House의 건축가가 돛단배에서 영감을 얻었다고 생각한다.', '사람들은 Dongdaemun Design Plaza가 거대한 행성을 닮았다고 생각한다.']);
add(R, passage(P, SYDDP101, `위 글의 빈칸 ⓐ~ⓔ에 들어갈 단어로 바르게 연결되지 않은 것은?`), '3', ['ⓐ: easily', 'ⓑ: obvious', 'ⓒ: far away', 'ⓓ: clearly', 'ⓔ: attraction']);
const SAG103 = `ⓐ<u>Nature has inspired many architects around the world</u>. ⓑ<u>This is the Sagrada Familia in Spain</u>. ⓒ<u>It is one of the (A)[most famous / famousest] churches in the world</u>. ⓓ<u>Look at the beautiful tall columns inside the church</u>. ⓔ<u>They look like trees, (B)[don't / do] they</u>? The famous architect, Antoni Gaudi, used the shape of trees in the Sagrada Familia. That's (C)[how / the way how] he brought the beauty of nature ㉠<u>indoors</u>.`;
add(R, passage(P, SAG103, `위 글의 밑줄 친 ㉠에 해당하는 곳에 대한 구체적 설명이 포함된 문장은?`), '4', CIRCLE);
add(R, passage(P, SAG103, `위 글의 괄호 (A), (B), (C)에서 어법상 알맞은 말로 바르게 연결된 것은?`), '2', ["(A) most famous – (B) don't – (C) the way how", "(A) most famous – (B) don't – (C) how", "(A) famousest – (B) don't – (C) how", '(A) most famous – (B) do – (C) how', '(A) famousest – (B) do – (C) the way how']);
add(R, `다음 글의 밑줄 친 ⓐ~ⓔ 중, 어법상 어색한 것은?\n\nIn the first two examples, we can ⓐ<u>easily</u> see what inspired the architect. But in the next example from Australia, this is not so ⓑ<u>obviously</u>. Jørn Utzon, the architect of the Sydney Opera House, took a shape from nature and added his imagination. Can you guess what inspired him? Many people think that it is the waves in the ocean or a sailing boat. But ⓒ<u>interestingly</u>, the inspiration came from an orange. Look at the roof ⓓ<u>closely</u>. Can you see the peels of an orange? When orange lights are shone on the building, you can see the peels more ⓔ<u>clearly</u>.`, '2', CIRCLE);
const DDP106 = `What about Korea? Have you ever (A)[gone / been] to Dongdaemun Design Plaza in Seoul? Many people think that the building (B)[looks like / looks] a giant spaceship. But the architect, Zaha Hadid, took the curved lines from nature so that city people could enjoy them. Thanks (C)[to / for] its special design, it has become a popular tourist attraction in Seoul. As you can see, many buildings try (D)[capturing / to capture] the beauty of nature in their design. They are perfect examples of "Nature meets city." If you were an architect, what should you choose from nature?`;
add(R, passage(P, DDP106, `위 글의 괄호 (A)~(D)에서 어법상 알맞은 말로 바르게 연결된 것은?`), '3', ['been - looks - to - capturing', 'gone - looks - for - to capture', 'been - looks like - to - to capture', 'gone - looks like - for - capturing', 'been - looks like - for - to capture']);
add(R, passage(P, DDP106, `위 글을 읽고 답할 수 없는 질문은?`), '2', ['Where is Dongdaemun Design Plaza?', 'What is Dongdaemun Design Plaza used for?', 'What does Dongdaemun Design Plaza look like?', 'Who is the architect of Dongdaemun Design Plaza?', 'What did the architect of Dongdaemun Design Plaza bring from nature?']);
const ARTSAG108 = `Have you ⓐ<u>hear</u> of the expression, "(A)______________________"? A lot of artists get their ideas and ⓑ<u>inspirations</u> from the world around them. This is ⓒ<u>because</u> the natural world is a beautiful place. The shapes in nature ⓓ<u>is</u> very ⓔ<u>pleasing</u> to the eye. For example, look at the egg on the left. Isn't it beautiful? It is round and delicate, yet (B)<u>그것의 내용물을 보호할 만큼 충분히 튼튼한</u>. Can you imagine a building that looks like an egg? ⓕ<u>A such building</u> actually exists in London.\nNature has inspired many architects around the world. This is the Sagrada Familia in Spain. It is ⓖ<u>one of the most famous church</u> in the world. Look at the beautiful tall columns inside the church. They look like trees, ⓗ<u>do they</u>? The famous architect, Antoni Gaudi, ⓘ<u>was used</u> the shape of trees in the Sagrada Familia. That's ⓙ<u>what</u> he brought the beauty of nature indoors.`;
